<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Personal Success Story</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> Saving for Future Goals!</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Interpret dialogues from various social contexts. Interactive: Highlight positive and negative opinions related to an audio or video recording during discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can interpret dialogues about achieving personal goals, relating them to real-life examples (e.g., "The speaker shared how a scholarship from IFARHU helped him/her achieve his/her educational goals while saving money for other priorities.")
                Can highlight diverse opinions in audio stories about saving money and planning toward future goals (e.g., "I've saved part of my allowance for a new laptop, but some of my friends find it hard to save because they spend their money on other things.")
                Can ask questions to clarify ideas in success stories (e.g., "What strategies has the speaker used to overcome challenges?" "The speaker mentioned that he/she asked friends for support, which helped him/her stay motivated during tough times.")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>
</div>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images related to future goals (e.g., a person graduating, a savings account statement, a new laptop, a small business logo, a travel destination). Students, in pairs, briefly discuss what these images represent and why people would want to *save* for them. The teacher elicits initial ideas and introduces the Theme: "Saving for Future Goals!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces 5-6 core vocabulary words for the lesson, connecting them to the images: <i>savings, goals, investment, career, achievements, opportunities</i>. The teacher says each word clearly, models its pronunciation, and provides a simple definition and context. For example, for "savings," "This is money you put aside for the *future*." The teacher explicitly models stressing content words, like "<b>SAV</b>ings," "<b>GOALS</b>," and "in<b>VEST</b>ment," and explains the importance of this for natural rhythm.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the vocabulary using concept checking questions and examples.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you want to buy a car next year, is that a short-term or a long-term goal?" (Expected answer: "Short-term goal.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If someone has a lot of 'savings,' do they have a lot of money in the bank or do they spend it all?" (Expected answer: "They have a lot of money in the bank.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What's an 'achievement' you are proud of from this school year?" (Elicit personal examples to contextualize the word.)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Personal Success Story"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording of a short interview (dialogue, ~1.5-2 minutes) with a young Panamanian entrepreneur sharing their "personal success story" related to *saving for future goals*. The dialogue incorporates the target vocabulary (<i>success, goals, savings, investment, career, achievements, opportunities, strategies, future</i>) and grammar (present perfect, future continuous, first conditional). Example phrases include: "I have saved some money for my college studies," "In five years, I will be managing my own company," "If I continue my studies, I will find more opportunities."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Interpret dialogues from various social contexts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Listening (Gist):</b> Students listen to the audio once. They answer general questions:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the speaker's main *achievement* mentioned in the interview?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What are they currently *saving* money for?"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Listening (True/False):</b> Students listen again and complete a True/False exercise based on specific details from the audio. E.g., "The speaker has already invested in a new business. (True/False)" "Their *future* plan involves studying abroad. (True/False)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third Listening (Vocabulary Matching):</b> Students listen a third time. They are given a list of target vocabulary words (e.g., *finances, strategies, opportunities*) and must match them to short phrases or examples heard in the audio that illustrate their meaning.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Focusing on Listening for details. Grammar: Present perfect, Future continuous, First conditional. Vocabulary: <i>success, goals, finances, savings, investment, career, achievements, opportunities, strategies, future</i>. Pronunciation: Stressing content words and reducing stress on auxiliary verbs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listening for Grammar & Pronunciation):</b> The teacher plays specific sentences from the audio that use the target grammar. Students complete cloze sentences or fill-in-the-gap exercises on their worksheets.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 1: "I ______ (save) some money for my college studies." (Students write "have saved"). The teacher replays the sentence, focusing on the stress pattern: "I have <b>SAVED</b> some <b>MO</b>ney for my <b>COL</b>lege <b>STU</b>dies," highlighting the reduced stress on "have" (həv).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 2: "In five years, I ______ (manage) my own company." (Students write "will be managing"). The teacher replays, emphasizing "In <b>FIVE YEARS</b>, I will be <b>MA</b>naging my <b>OWN COM</b>pany," and reducing stress on "will" (wəl).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Listening for Opinions):</b> The teacher replays portions of the audio where the speaker expresses positive or negative opinions about different saving *strategies* or *challenges*. Students work in pairs to identify phrases indicating positive or negative sentiment. For example, "It's been a real *challenge*," (negative) or "I've seen great *growth*," (positive). They then share one positive and one negative opinion they *heard*.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Listening for Clarification):</b> Students are given specific scenarios related to *saving* or *achievements* (e.g., "A friend mentions they can't save money because they spend it on clothes"). In pairs, students formulate questions they would ask to *clarify* the situation or offer advice, practicing the pragmatic function of engaging in conversations on goals and aspirations. They then listen to their partner's questions, providing feedback on clarity and relevance. E.g., "What *strategies* has the speaker used to overcome challenges?" "The speaker mentioned that he/she asked friends for support, which helped him/her stay motivated during tough times."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Personal Success Story"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small Group Discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher divides students into groups of 3-4. Each group receives a brief audio clip (30-45 seconds) of another student or young adult sharing a specific challenge or dilemma related to *saving money* or pursuing a *long-term goal* (e.g., "I've saved part of my allowance for a new laptop, but some of my friends find it hard to save because they spend their money on other things.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students *listen* carefully to the audio clip individually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Within their groups, students discuss their interpretations and initial opinions about the speaker's situation, focusing on highlighting both positive and negative aspects or *strategies* they heard or would suggest. They actively *listen* to each other's contributions, practicing respectful discussion of personal achievements and goals (Pragmatic competence).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group should collaboratively *listen* and formulate a collective response that demonstrates diverse opinions about the speaker's challenge and potential *future* *opportunities*. They are encouraged to use the target grammar (present perfect, future continuous, first conditional) and vocabulary introduced. This stage also encourages leading group discussions, ensuring all voices are heard and respected (Sociolinguistic competence).</li>
                </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussions and a quick group feedback session.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly shares with the class one key positive and one key negative opinion or *strategy* they identified from the audio clip and discussed. The teacher will listen to their summary and the clarity of their opinions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to interpret dialogues and identify main ideas/opinions from the audio.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and intelligibility of spoken summary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>savings, goals, strategies</i>) and grammar (e.g., present perfect for achievements, future continuous for goals).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstration of active listening and respectful interaction within the group.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effort to stress content words and reduce auxiliary stress in spoken output.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket with the following prompts:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word or phrase related to 'Saving for Future Goals!' that I understood well today was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of listening to today's success stories did I find most challenging, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What *strategies* for interpreting dialogues and identifying opinions did I use today that helped me understand the audio clips?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can *listening* to others' *success stories* or challenges related to *saving* help me think about my own *future goals*?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What worked well in this lesson, especially regarding students' ability to interpret dialogues and highlight opinions?" (e.g., clarity of audio, engagement with vocabulary).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next listening tasks or for students struggling with identifying specific details or opinions?" (e.g., providing transcripts, pre-teaching more vocabulary, more repetition).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension), students will engage with a written text that further explores the theme of "Saving for Future Goals!" and "My Personal Success Story," reinforcing the vocabulary and grammatical structures introduced in today's listening activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>